Attribution correction.

This commit's message describes only the §6 rewrite. Its diff carries three
further hunk groups that another author wrote and that the message does not
mention:

  -191  §4 Frozen  "All four are archived on GitHub and read-only."
  -229  §5 table   Binaries row -> "release manifest (signable; unsigned in CI)"
  -238  §5 prose   the "Three notes" paragraph, replaced wholesale

Those three are the work of the agent operating as `vela-epoch`, not of the
author of this message. The §5 table correction in particular is theirs and is
the substantive catch: the row had said "signed release manifest" while CI
passes no --sign-key and ships it unsigned.

How it happened: several agents were writing to one shared working tree, and
this commit staged `docs/ECOSYSTEM.md` by explicit path while that file also
held their uncommitted edits. `git add <path>` takes whatever is in the tree at
that path regardless of who put it there, so naming the file was no protection.
The lesson is not "avoid git add -A" — it is that a shared index cannot
attribute work, and separate worktrees are the only fix.

Recorded as a note rather than by rewriting a pushed commit.
